---
import LegalPage from "../components/LegalPage.astro";
import { GITHUB_REPOSITORY_URL } from "../lib/site";

const sections = [
  ["controller", "Controller"],
  ["short-version", "Short version"],
  ["website", "This website"],
  ["app", "The Kassiber app"],
  ["outbound", "Connections the app can make"],
  ["email", "Email and GitHub"],
  ["rights", "Your rights"],
  ["changes", "Changes"],
] as const;

const SECURITY_MD_URL = `${GITHUB_REPOSITORY_URL}/blob/main/SECURITY.md`;
---

<LegalPage
  title="Privacy Policy · Kassiber"
  description="What this website and the Kassiber app do with data. There is no Kassiber server, no account, and no telemetry."
  heading="Privacy Policy"
  lede="Kassiber is free software that runs on your machine. There is no Kassiber account, no Kassiber server, and no telemetry, so this policy is short: the little that happens on this website, and the connections the app makes when you ask it to."
  effectiveDate="2026-07-29"
  lastUpdated="2026-07-29"
  sections={sections}
>
  <section id="controller">
    <p class="section-number">01</p>
    <h2>Controller</h2>
    <address>
      <strong>Bitcoin Austria &ndash; Verein zur F&ouml;rderung von Bitcoin in &Ouml;sterreich</strong><br />
      c/o STADLER PARTNER Rechtsanw&auml;lte GmbH<br />
      Seilerst&auml;tte 24, 1010 Vienna, Austria
    </address>
    <a class="contact-link" href="mailto:office@bitcoin-austria.at">office@bitcoin-austria.at</a>
    <p>
      Full association details are in the{" "}<a href="/imprint">imprint</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="short-version">
    <p class="section-number">02</p>
    <h2>Short Version</h2>
    <p>
      We don't track you. This website sets no cookies and runs no analytics. The app phones nobody:
      your wallets, transactions, and reports stay in a database file on your own disk, and every
      connection it makes is one you configured. There is no Kassiber cloud to breach, subpoena, or
      sell. That is the point of the project, and it is why this policy is short.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="website">
    <p class="section-number">03</p>
    <h2>This Website</h2>
    <p>
      The site is served by Vercel (Vercel Inc., USA), which receives your IP address, access time,
      and browser type as part of delivering the page (Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR;{" "}<a
        href="https://vercel.com/legal/privacy-notice">Vercel's privacy notice</a
      >). Fonts are self-hosted, and there are no cookies, analytics, pixels, or ads.
    </p>
    <p>
      One exception: the{" "}<a href="/download">download page</a> asks GitHub's public API for the
      latest release so it can offer you the right file. Your browser makes that request directly,
      so GitHub sees your IP and that a release list was requested (Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR;{" "}<a
        href="https://docs.github.com/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement"
        >GitHub's privacy statement</a
      >). Nothing else is embedded or preloaded; other sites hear from you only when you click a
      link to them.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="app">
    <p class="section-number">04</p>
    <h2>The Kassiber App</h2>
    <p>
      Kassiber is software you install and run yourself, not a service we operate. There is no
      account to create, no telemetry or crash reporting, and no server of ours holding your
      addresses, descriptors, balances, or identity. It is watch-only: it never asks for private
      keys and never builds, signs, or broadcasts a transaction.
    </p>
    <p>
      Your books live in a SQLite database under <code>~/.kassiber</code>, optionally encrypted with
      a passphrase only you know. Backups, sync, and deletion are all in your hands; we could not
      delete or recover your data if we wanted to. Optional device/team sync replicates through
      storage <em>you</em> choose, with no Kassiber server involved. Boundaries and caveats:
      {" "}<a href={SECURITY_MD_URL}>SECURITY.md</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="outbound">
    <p class="section-number">05</p>
    <h2>Connections the App Can Make</h2>
    <p>
      Every outbound request is enumerated in{" "}<a href={SECURITY_MD_URL}>SECURITY.md</a>, which
      is the authoritative version of this section. In short:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Update checks (opt-in).</strong> If enabled under
        {" "}<strong>Settings &rarr; Privacy</strong>, Kassiber asks GitHub whether a newer release
        exists. GitHub sees your IP and the app version, and no project, wallet, device, or
        installation identifier. Nothing is ever downloaded or installed automatically.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Chain backends.</strong> Syncing a wallet queries the backend you selected. Your own
        node means nothing leaves your network; a remote backend sees your IP and the addresses
        being queried. The built-in defaults are Bitcoin Austria's own
        {" "}<a href="https://mempool.bitcoin-austria.at">mempool.bitcoin-austria.at</a> and Fulcrum
        (for Liquid: Bull Bitcoin and Blockstream). Any backend can be routed over Tor.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Exchange rates.</strong> Fetching rates contacts your configured provider (Coinbase
        by default), which sees your IP and the currency pair. A bundled offline price history lets
        you skip this entirely.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>AI assistant.</strong> Defaults to a local model, where nothing leaves your machine.
        A remote provider must be explicitly acknowledged first, and then sees your prompts and
        attached accounting context under its own terms. We receive none of it and train nothing on
        anything.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Where a Bitcoin Austria backend is involved, we receive that connection data to operate the
      service, not to profile you, and we recommend swapping in your own node. Third-party backends
      and providers process data under their own policies.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="email">
    <p class="section-number">06</p>
    <h2>Email and GitHub</h2>
    <p>
      When you email us, we process your message to handle your inquiry (Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR, or
      Art. 6(1)(b) for contract-related inquiries). Our email runs on Google Workspace (Google
      Ireland Ltd.), so messages are stored on Google servers; Google is certified under the EU-U.S.
      Data Privacy Framework (<a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy"
        >policies.google.com/privacy</a
      >). Please don't send descriptors, xpubs, seed words, or keys.
    </p>
    <p>
      Kassiber is developed in public on{" "}<a href={GITHUB_REPOSITORY_URL}>GitHub</a>. If you open
      or comment on an issue, you are using that platform directly under its own privacy statement,
      and whatever you post is public. Redact transaction details first.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="rights">
    <p class="section-number">07</p>
    <h2>Your Rights</h2>
    <p>
      You have the rights of access, rectification, erasure, restriction, data portability, and
      objection. Write to{" "}<a href="mailto:office@bitcoin-austria.at"
        >office@bitcoin-austria.at</a
      >, and you may lodge a complaint with the Austrian Data Protection Authority
      (<a href="https://www.dsb.gv.at">dsb.gv.at</a>). In practice there is usually little for us to
      act on, since the data in your books never reaches us. For data held by a backend, rate
      provider, or AI provider you configured, ask that operator.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="changes">
    <p class="section-number">08</p>
    <h2>Changes</h2>
    <p>
      We update this policy when what the software or the website does changes; the current version
      applies from the date above. Kassiber is free software, so you can verify every claim here
      against the source and{" "}<a href={SECURITY_MD_URL}>SECURITY.md</a> instead of taking our
      word for it.
    </p>
  </section>
</LegalPage>
